Submission docs, written from the repo and asserted by tests

docs/DEVPOST.md, docs/DEMO_SCRIPT.md and a rewritten README, all grounded in
what the code actually does.

tests/test_claims.py recomputes every number in the judged prose from the code
rather than trusting the sentence. On its FIRST run it caught two stale claims
of my own:

  prose said 73 tests, suite collected 90
  prose credited core/synthesis with 12 tests, it has 16

Both were true when written and went stale silently, which is the exact failure
this test exists to prevent. It also proves the headline claim rather than
repeating it: it opens the 532-page PDF and asserts that "5,000 cells" appears
zero times, and it cross-checks the recorded run's cited pages, the verified
citation count, the closure trigger constant, the FWC band edges, the computed
ruler position and the live URL.

It blocks the specific fabrications that kept reappearing in AI-suggested
submission copy: Firestore, Vertex AI, Cloud Storage, decibel computation,
"threshold breach", and "202 Accepted". None are used and several are the
opposite of the finding.

TWO OF MY OWN TESTS WERE WRONG AND I FIXED THE TESTS, NOT THE CODE:

1. The forbidden-term check flagged the README's honest sentence "has no
   decibel field, by construction", which is the opposite of a fabrication.
   A blunt substring check cannot tell a claim from its denial. It now allows
   a sentence carrying a negation to name the thing it denies.

2. The test-count check grabbed every "N tests" in the prose and tripped on
   the decision layer's own separate count. Split into a total-suite claim and
   a decision-layer claim, each verified against its own collection. A claim
   test that cannot tell two claims apart produces false failures and then gets
   disabled, which is worse than not having it.

The demo script carries an explicit "do not say" list: never call the recorded
run live, never say crisis or critical when the finding is that the map is
ambiguous, never say decibel or threshold breach, never name a Google product
the project does not use.

91 tests plus 22 route assertions pass.
